As a by now complete outsider (haven't touched NH in years), here are some 
observations:

1. I understand that you (and some others) are unhappy about the lack of 
(visible) progress. Note that complaining will not help. (It won't help 
even for more transparent production processes, but in particular it won't 
help at all with the NH devteam.)

2. There is no reason to believe the NH devteam will hand the imprimatur 
to anyone. Simply don't ask for it -- they won't even reply.

3. I think that most r.g.r.n. readers would be happy about a new version. 
But as you will note, nobody supports regicide (especially by someone 
unarmed like you).


> Please, no retorts of "do it yourself then!", because I'll ignore them
> as usual.  The argument is invalid.

It is not. The argument is fully valid. You are not a developer but you 
can still do something.

4. Play the variants. By this I mostly mean you should play Sporkhack. 
Give feedback on this newsgroup. First, good ideas from Spork may go into 
mainline Nethack -- something like this has been seen before. 
Second, someone already stepped up and did something. By providing 
feedback and criticism, you keep Spork in the public focus and support a 
live fork.

5. Even non-coders can help. (I am a non-coding Crawl developer and we 
made a point to have non-coding players be able to help: design vaults, 
add speech etc.) The ones to ask would be Derek or Pasi, not the devteam.

6. Just to make sure: play the variants or test the patches. Report about 
that. (Even if you were going to collect a team of willing coders, you'd 
need to do that. Could just as well start with it.) As far as I 
understand, you only tool are words. So write about cool features and 
ideas, and try to evaluate them from a design point of view (not just like 
a player).


> 5 years, people.

What now follows is a personal rant. You may ignore it; I probably won't 
reply.
The worst aspect about the devteam's silence is ignorance: There have been 
a huge number of contributions of all scales. While I (really!) understand 
the need to be reluctant about adding new features, it would be a sign of 
good leadership to explain why patches are (not) used. With their current 
approach, lots of efforts go to waste.
Also, it is normal that people move on and lose interest. I am convinced 
that a closed and cabalistic approach works in the early stages but not 
for grownup projects. The devteam has added patchers to the rooster in the 
past, so that's a sign of some hope. I wonder if they ever approached the 
Sporkhack maintainers...

David


